WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.780
Hi, I'm Kumudika, a product designer on
Atlassian's Data Experiences team, and I

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.000
wanted to share an early exploration of
how we design charts for communication.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.290
Our existing charts support analytical
work, and in our library revamp earlier

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:19.328
this year, we also did explore
informational charts to make insights more

00:00:19.328 --> 00:00:23.400
approachable for people without
deep technical expertise.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.640
Seeing these two side-by-side did
expose a gap, where we found that there's

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.813
a need for more expressive charts that
help people communicate a specific

00:00:31.813 --> 00:00:37.960
message or tell a story with data. This is where narrative charts came in.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.360
We studied the competitive landscape
and explored how much expression

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.726
different contexts need. We mapped
out an expressive matrix from restrained

00:00:47.726 --> 00:00:53.020
and data forward to bold and story forward
rather than imposing one visual tone

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.360
on every message. That led to
these three experimental styles, Tufte,

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:05.156
Charlie, and Sumi, where Tufte is more
minimal, quiet, and it has a serious tone

00:01:05.156 --> 00:01:10.790
for technical, restrained communication.
Charlie is for a friendlier tone,

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.560
more approachable voice, which
uses our branding colors.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:21.190
Finally, Sumi is high contrast and
expressive, so this is useful for when

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:28.880
our customers need a story that demands
a bolder expression, like this here.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.320
Defining these styles
was only half the problem.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.720
Most people have limited time, and turning
data into a clear story takes skill

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.480
that most don't have time for. This
is why we started exploring AI-generated

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.884
charts, to lower that barrier with the
core principle that AI proposes and our

00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:53.417
defined system helps to constrain. We're currently exploring a prototype

00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:59.046
where you supply your data, you state
your goal, and AI will turn that into a

00:01:59.046 --> 00:02:02.740
first draft, and then you could
refine it however you like.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.640
AI here does not make
up numbers or visuals.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.480
It will still work within our defined
narrative chart system, so every draft

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.800
will follow the same rules,
which you could see here.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.580
You can see that these are early
explorations, not a final product, and

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.713
we're still learning how to make
our data storytelling easier without

00:02:24.713 --> 00:02:29.160
compromising trust or people's
control over these results.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.160
We'll share more as we test and
iterate, but this was an overview

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.320
of what we've been working on. Thank you so much for watching!

